"""Continuation of the manually authored claims; executed by build_claims.py."""
C(265,[244],47,[3], '2086–2117', 'F_A(x)=q_!(p^*(x)·P)',
  'For an abelian scheme A/S over a locally noetherian base and its dual B, the normalized Poincaré bundle defines Φ_A(M)=Rq_*(Lp^*M⊗P) on bounded coherent derived categories; properness and finite Tor dimension give the indicated K-group homomorphism. The rational Chow transform uses exp(c₁P) and proper pushforward.',
  'construction stated without full derived-category justification', 'computations.md §10 separates derived, K-theory and Chow conventions', 'B',
  'Laumon1996 §1.2, especially1.2.2–1.2.4, gives the relative functor, base change, coherence and inversion. Beauville1983 §1 defines the rational Chow operation and compares it with Mukai. Exact primary passages opened/read.',
  'Mukai1981 original derived transform; Laumon earlier manuscript September1985, arXiv5March1996; Beauville1983',
  'Established under these properness/base hypotheses. Cote47 is an external-context Fourier draft, not silently folded into the standard-conjecture proof.', ['Laumon1996','Beauville1983'])
C(266,[244],47,[3], '2108–2135', 'F(x*y)=F(x)F(y)',
  'The Poincaré biextension identity converts derived Pontryagin convolution into derived tensor product, and hence the corresponding K-theory products. Over a field the rational Chow Fourier transform sends Pontryagin product to intersection product without an extra parity sign in this direction.',
  'proof sketched by the Poincaré relation', 'computations.md §10 gives the kernel and projection-formula calculation', 'B',
  'Laumon1996 Proposition1.3.2 and formula1.3.3 give the derived statement. Beauville1983 Proposition3(ii), pp.243–244, gives both Chow directions, with the sign only in the reverse direction. Kleiman1968 §2A.12 states precisely the sign-free forward direction.',
  'Kleiman1968; Mukai1981; Beauville1983; relative exposition1985/1996',
  'Established; the allegation that Kleiman omitted a sign in this displayed direction is unsupported.', ['Laumon1996','Beauville1983','Kleiman1968'])
C(267,[245],47,[3], '2137–2154', 'Autres cas ... groupes plats ... support propre',
  'The margin asks how far the kernel construction extends from abelian schemes to more general flat group schemes if support is proper. Proper support permits relevant direct images, but a Fourier equivalence also needs a specified dual object, biextension, coherence and an inversion theorem; none is stated for every flat group scheme.',
  'open on the pages as a possible generalization', 'No computation supplies the missing duality hypotheses', 'C',
  'Laumon1996 develops generalized Fourier transforms beyond abelian schemes in later sections, while §1 treats the abelian case with exact hypotheses. These constructions do not assert an equivalence for every flat group scheme with an unspecified proper-support condition. Searches web003 and primary reading identify the distinction.',
  'Relative and generalized treatment1985/1996',
  'Partly developed in specified categories; the blanket marginal formulation is too incomplete for a universal theorem.', ['Laumon1996'], 'medium')
C(268,[246,248],47,[4,5], '2157–2278', 'F_B F_A ... (−1)^g ... τ_A',
  'For an abelian scheme of relative dimension g, the derived composite is canonically isomorphic to (−1_A)^*(−)⊗π_A^*τ_A[−g], where τ_A=det Lie(A/S)=ω_(A/S)|_e^−1. On K₀ this becomes (−1)^g[τ_A](−1_A)^*. A formula over a general base omitting the determinant line requires an additional trivialization or restriction to a point.',
  'central theorem proposed; proof reduced to the zero-section kernel calculation', 'C12 for the derived self-intersection; computations.md §10 for the convolution kernel and shift-to-sign rule', 'B',
  'Laumon1996 Theorem1.2.4 and Lemma1.2.5 prove exactly the relative determinant-line formula; Moonen2026 §2.3.1 writes the same normalization. The draft is earlier than Mukai’s1981 published theorem, but no full proof on these leaves is claimed.',
  'Mukai1981; Laumon1985 manuscript/1996 arXiv; Moonen first online2024, issue2026',
  'Established with the determinant line retained. Field-level inversion is the specialization of this formula.', ['Laumon1996','Moonen2026'])
C(269,[247],47,[4,5], '2180–2226;2227–2246', 'τ_A et τ_B canoniquement isomorphes',
  'The top invariant-differential lines of dual abelian schemes, and hence their inverse lines τ_A and τ_B, are canonically isomorphic. This does not identify the whole tangent bundles of A and B and does not canonically trivialize the common line.',
  'self-flagged step: canonical isomorphism asserted with a question', 'computations.md §10: determinant of R¹π_*O and relative Serre duality', 'B',
  'Laumon1996 §1.1.3 proves ω_A≅ω_B using R¹π_*O≅Lie(B) and relative duality. The exact line-bundle statement is therefore settled, unlike a stronger tangent-space identification.',
  'Mukai1981 framework; relative proof circulated1985, arXiv1996',
  'Established for abelian schemes over the locally noetherian base of the source.', ['Laumon1996'])
C(270,[251],47,[5,6], '2235–2332', 'Rp_*(P) ... e_*(τ)[−g]',
  'The derived direct image of the normalized Poincaré bundle along A×_S B→B is e_{B*}τ_A[−g]. In particular the cohomology sheaves vanish except in degree g, where the direct image is the zero-section sheaf with its determinant-line coefficient.',
  'open proof reduced to a canonical map and a fibre calculation', 'C12: derived pullback of e_*τ[−g] has all expected Tor terms, so ordinary base change is inadequate', 'B',
  'Laumon1996 Lemma1.2.5 is the exact relative formula; it explicitly recalls the canonical-map construction and points to the proof in the earlier Fourier work. Moonen2026 §2.4 uses it with the same line convention.',
  'Mukai1981; Laumon1985/1996',
  'Established. The apparent conflict with nonzero H^i(A,O_A) at the identity disappears only with derived pullback.', ['Laumon1996','Moonen2026'])
C(271,[249],47,[5,6], '2247–2332', 'L≠O ... H^i(A,L)=0 [Mumford]',
  'For an abelian variety over a field and a nontrivial algebraically trivial line bundle L, all H^i(A,L) vanish. This fibre statement is used in locating the support of the Poincaré direct image; by itself it does not determine its scheme structure or determinant line.',
  'invoked as a theorem of Mumford without proof', 'No numerical computation; the statement is checked against the exact Fourier-kernel result', 'E',
  'Mumford’s Abelian Varieties is the named historical source; the supplied scanned PartII copy was obtained but the exact lemma page was not text-verifiable. Laumon1996 Lemma1.2.5 implies the stated field-level vanishing by derived base change, so the mathematical assertion is verified independently of that unresolved page locator.',
  'Mumford theory before these Fourier notes; precise relative formulation1985/1996',
  'Established. Historical page attribution to Mumford remains qualified; no inaccessible theorem number is supplied.', ['MumfordAbelianII','Laumon1996'])
C(272,[250],47,[6,7], '2279–2379', 'vérifier ... isomorphisme',
  'The candidate map from the Poincaré direct image to the zero-section complex must agree under base change with the top-cohomology/duality map and be an isomorphism, including at the identity. Pointwise support alone does not verify this map.',
  'self-flagged compatibility and isomorphism check', 'computations.md §10 spells out why cone vanishing plus derived base change is the needed check, not equality of supports', 'B',
  'Laumon1996 proof of Lemma1.2.5 recalls construction from the R^g direct-image base-change isomorphism and the canonical-map truncation. It cites the established Fourier theorem for the isomorphism; this run does not claim an independent full geometric proof from the leaf.',
  'Mukai1981; relative exposition1985/1996',
  'Settled as part of the relative Fourier inversion theorem; manuscript proof remains incomplete at this step.', ['Laumon1996'])
C(273,[252],47,[7], '2333–2379', 'changement de base',
  'For the derived Poincaré transform, arbitrary base change is expressed using Lψ^*, not ordinary ψ^*. At a zero section of relative dimension g, L e^*e_*O has Tor_i≅∧^i e^*Ω, so replacing derived pullback by underived pullback loses the cohomology needed by the calculation.',
  'proof sketched with base-change notation whose derived meaning matters', 'C12 exact Koszul ranks; computations.md §10 gives the regular-immersion Koszul proof', 'B',
  'Laumon1996 §1.2.2 is the exact arbitrary-base-change statement for the relative transform. The module-level Koszul computation is re-proved here; no flatness of an arbitrary base change is assumed.',
  'Mukai1981 framework; relative exposition1985/1996',
  'Established in the derived formulation. Ordinary pullback is valid under an added Tor-independence condition.', ['Laumon1996'])
C(274,[253],47,[7,8], '2333–2419', 'F_A(δ_s)=P_s; F_A(P_t)=...δ_(−t)',
  'A point section class transforms to the corresponding Poincaré line bundle. Conversely the line P_t transforms to τ_A[−g] supported at −t on the dual; the inversion is part of the chosen normalized Poincaré convention.',
  'computed on the page; a later summary drops the minus sign', 'computations.md §10 derives the second formula from inversion and the first, fixing signs', 'B',
  'Laumon1996 Theorem1.2.4 and functoriality1.3.1 yield these exact transforms. Beauville1983 Proposition4 gives the associated translation identities in Chow theory.',
  'Mukai1981; Beauville1983; relative exposition1985/1996',
  'Established with −t and the determinant-line factor. The later unsigned δ_t cannot be read literally with the earlier convention.', ['Laumon1996','Beauville1983'])
C(275,[],47,[8], '2380–2419', 'εF=χ; χF=(−1)^gε',
  'Over a field, rank and Euler characteristic of a K-class are exchanged by Fourier transform: rank Φ(x)=χ(x), and χ(Φ(x))=(−1)^g rank(x). The determinant line has rank1; the sign comes from the shift.',
  'computed without full proof in the list of formulas', 'computations.md §10 proves the first by fibrewise Euler characteristic and the second by applying inversion', 'B',
  'Laumon1996 inversion and base-change formulas establish the two equalities; Beauville1983 Proposition2 compares the K- and Chow transforms. The statement is over a field, not an unqualified integer-valued Euler characteristic over every base.',
  'Mukai1981; Beauville1983',
  'Established with the base and Euler-characteristic convention stated.', ['Laumon1996','Beauville1983'])
C(276,[],47,[8,9], '2380–2471', 'δ′δ=−χ·id',
  'For a nondegenerate line bundle defining an isogeny δ:A→B and Euler characteristic χ≠0, the determinant of its Fourier transform defines the signed dual isogeny δ′ with δ′δ=−χ·id, in the manuscript Poincaré convention. The negative sign is tied to det Φ(L), rather than to a separately chosen positive polarization.',
  'computed/formally asserted in the line-bundle formulas', 'computations.md §10 derives the sign from ch Φ(L)=χ exp(−D′/χ); C07 checks the surface specialization', 'B',
  'Beauville1983 Proposition5, pp.247–248, treats the Fourier image of exp(d) and the dual polarization; Laumon1996 fixes the derived convention. These sources must be compared with the page’s signed δ′, not used to remove its sign by naming it a polarization.',
  'Mukai1981; Beauville1983',
  'Established for nondegenerate line bundles with χ nonzero. Literal inverse-isogeny formulas require rational coefficients or the stated scalar multiple.', ['Beauville1983','Laumon1996'])
C(277,[254],47,[9], '2420–2471', 'ch F(L)=χ exp(−D′/χ)',
  'With D′ the appropriate dual divisor class and χ=χ(L) nonzero, the Chern character of the Fourier K-class is χ exp(−D′/χ), in the manuscript’s convention. Consequently c_t(ΦL)=(1−D′t/χ)^χ and c_k=(−1)^k[χ(χ−1)…(χ−k+1)/χ^k]D′^k/k!.',
  'computation on the page; its expanded coefficient has an extra factor', 'C07 symbolic coefficient calculation and explicit abelian-surface example; computations.md §10 gives the logarithmic Chern identity', 'B',
  'Beauville1983 Proposition5 gives the Fourier exponential formula; the Chern conversion is a direct formal identity re-derived here. The printed expansion in the manuscript is separately refuted by CL-SA07-278.',
  'Mukai1981; Beauville1983; direct recomputation2026',
  'Established with χ≠0 and consistent signed divisor convention.', ['Beauville1983'])
C(278,[255],47,[9], '2454–2471', 'χ ... χ(χ−1)...',
  'The literal expanded Chern coefficient has one extra χ. Take A=E₁×E₂ and L of bidegree(1,2), so χ=2. Its Fourier image has rank2, D′²=4[pt], and c₂=1[pt]; the extra factor predicts2[pt]. All line bundles are nondegenerate and the characteristic-zero example satisfies the hypotheses.',
  'false as stated in the displayed expansion', 'C07 exact polynomial coefficient and bidegree computation; outputs retain the correction of an initial working D′² value to4', 'B',
  'Beauville1983 Proposition5 fixes the exponential formula; standard Chern identities then force the corrected coefficient. This is an arithmetic slip, not a new obstruction to Fourier inversion or the weaker divisibility consequence.',
  '1983 exact published formula; manuscript arithmetic checked2026',
  'Refuted literal coefficient; refined statement CL-SA07-277. No new ink reading is adopted.', ['Beauville1983'])
C(279,[257],47,[9], '2454–2471', 'donc ... intégral',
  'The weaker class χ(χ−1)…(χ−k+1)D′^k/(k!χ^(k−1)) equals (−1)^kχ c_k(ΦL). Thus it is integral whenever ΦL is an integral K-class, despite the extra factor error if the expression is called c_k itself.',
  'unpursued consequence of the Chern computation, with a stronger formula incorrectly written', 'C07 verifies the polynomial identity; computations.md §10 proves it symbolically', 'B',
  'Beauville1983 Proposition5 and the existence of the integral K-theory transform provide the exact inputs. The implication is re-proved here and does not assert integral Chow Fourier for every cycle.',
  'Mukai1981/Beauville1983 framework',
  'Established as the weaker integrality consequence. It survives the correction of CL-SA07-278.', ['Beauville1983','Laumon1996'])
C(280,[259],47,[10,11], '2472–2573', 'Riemann–Roch',
  'For a smooth abelian scheme with T_A=Td(Lie A) on the base, Grothendieck–Riemann–Roch gives ch(x*y)=T_A·(ch x*ch y) and ch Φ_A(x)=T_A·Ψ_A(ch x). These formulas keep the relative tangent Todd factor; over a field it is1.',
  'formal computation asserted on the page', 'computations.md §10 gives both pushforward derivations from the relative tangent bundles', 'B',
  'Moonen2026 §§2.3–2.4 checks the same relative Fourier/GRR normalization, and Beauville1983 Proposition2 is the field-level comparison. These are not evidence that every base Todd factor may simply be discarded.',
  'Beauville1983; relative refinements1985/1996 and2024/2026',
  'Established with the relative factors stated.', ['Moonen2026','Beauville1983'])
C(281,[258],47,[9,10,11], '2420–2573', 'mod torsion ?',
  'The question whether rational Chow Fourier preserves integral Chow classes modulo torsion for all abelian varieties has a negative answer. For a very general complex principally polarized abelian variety of dimension g≥4, Ψ(θ) is, up to sign, θ^(g−1)/(g−1)!, while no integral algebraic cycle has this minimal cohomology class.',
  'open on the pages as an integral/modulo-torsion question', 'computations.md §10: nonalgebraicity of the torsion-free cohomology class obstructs any integral Chow lift, so quotienting Chow torsion does not repair it', 'C',
  'Beckmann–de Gaay Fortman2023 Theorem3.8, pp.1197–1199, gives equivalent integral-lift criteria and asks the general PPAV question. Engel–de Gaay Fortman–Schreieder2025/2026 Theorem1.1, p.2, proves every codimension c cycle for2≤c≤g−1 on a very general PPAV has an even multiple of θ^c/c! as class; this refutes the unrestricted question. Kahn2026 Theorem1.2/Corollary1.3 gives an étale-Chow replacement after inverting exponential characteristic and quotienting the subgroup killed by2. The latter is a different cycle theory, not a repair within ordinary Chow.',
  'Criterion published15May2023; counterexample first circulated21July2025, v3March2026; étale variant first11February2026, v2June2026; no journal publication claimed for these preprints',
  'General ordinary integral/modulo-torsion statement refuted by current primary literature; positive special cases and the étale replacement remain distinct. Confidence concerns the precise theorem statement and implication, not an independent verification of the85-page proof.', ['BeckmannFortman2023','EngelFortmanSchreieder2025','Kahn2026'])
C(282,[260],47,[10,11], '2472–2573', 'F(xy)=...; Td(A)Td(B)...',
  'The derived reverse-product identity contains ω_A[g]; on K₀ it contributes (−1)^gω_A. After GRR the corresponding relative Chow factor is (−1)^g Td(Lie A)Td(Lie B)ch(ω_A), equivalently (−1)^g Td(H¹_dR(A/S)). Its simplification to (−1)^g requires a vanishing theorem for that Todd class.',
  'computation and a tentative simplification', 'computations.md §10 derives the factor and the dual-bundle Todd identity', 'B',
  'Laumon1996 formula1.3.3 proves the derived factor. Moonen2026 Corollary2.7(1) proves Td(H¹_dR)=1 for an abelian scheme over a connected smooth finite-type scheme over a Dedekind domain. Corollary2.7(2) makes stronger Chern-class vanishing conditional on an isomorphism of Hodge bundles, e.g. a separable isogeny to the dual.',
  'Laumon1985/1996; Moonen first online2024, journal issueJanuary2026',
  'Established factor; the cited Todd simplification is established under the explicit base hypotheses. It is not a theorem here about every possibly singular base.', ['Laumon1996','Moonen2026'])
C(283,[261,262],47,[10,11], '2472–2573', 'erreur ... Kleiman ?',
  'Over a field, Ψ_A(ab)=(−1)^g Ψ_A(a)*Ψ_A(b), whereas Ψ_A(a*b)=Ψ_A(a)Ψ_A(b). The manuscript’s concern about a parity sign is valid for the reverse direction; inspection of Kleiman’s §2A.12–2A.13 finds only the forward formula and no printed sign error in those propositions.',
  'self-flagged sign check and bibliographic allegation', 'computations.md §10 derives reverse sign by applying Ψ_BΨ_A=(−1)^g(−1)^*; C07 supplies an even-dimensional consistency check', 'B',
  'Beauville1983 Proposition3(ii), pp.243–244, states both directions exactly. Kleiman1968 §2A.12–2A.13, pp.377–378, was opened/read and supports the forward direction; the allegation cannot be confirmed by attributing a reverse formula he did not print there.',
  'Kleiman1968; explicit paired formulasBeauville1983',
  'Parity resolved; the specific alleged error in the cited Kleiman passage is not substantiated.', ['Beauville1983','Kleiman1968'])
C(284,[263],47,[10,11], '2472–2573', 'dualité',
  'Duality of Fourier transforms involves the dual Poincaré kernel P^∨, equivalently inversion on one factor, together with the relative dualizing line and dimension shift. A bare equality between ordinary duals of F(x) and F(x^∨) omitting these operations is not the canonical formula.',
  'stated as a compatibility to clarify; checked-transform notation is not fully defined', 'computations.md §10 derives the kernel dual from relative duality and records unresolved notation separately', 'B',
  'Laumon1996 Proposition1.3.4 gives the exact duality compatibility with the inversion morphism and shifts. This settles a correctly normalized version, not the meaning of the page’s undefined checked F by fiat.',
  'Relative exposition1985/1996, building onMukai1981',
  'Established after explicit normalization; the notation on the leaf still needs contextual identification.', ['Laumon1996'])
C(285,[],47,[11], '2528–2573', 'équivariance',
  'For a homomorphism f:A→B of abelian schemes, the Fourier operation exchanges pushforward along f with derived pullback along the dual homomorphism. In particular equivariance under an automorphism must use the contragredient dual action, with inverse if the group-action convention requires it.',
  'asserted in the summary of functorial formulas', 'computations.md §10 checks the direction from the Poincaré pullback identity', 'B',
  'Laumon1996 Proposition1.3.1 is the exact relative formula; Beauville1983 Proposition3(iii) gives the isogeny version in rational Chow theory.',
  'Mukai1981; Beauville1983; Laumon1985/1996',
  'Established with directions and variance retained.', ['Laumon1996','Beauville1983'])

C(286,[265],54,[3], '2629–2640', 'extension universelle ... V(t_B^∨)',
  'For an abelian variety A with dual B, vector extensions by a vector space V are classified by Hom(H¹(A,O_A)^∨,V). The universal vector extension E(A) has vector kernel H¹(A,O_A)^∨=Lie(B)^∨; its tangent sequence is0→Lie(B)^∨→Lie E(A)→Lie(A)→0.',
  'construction sketched using the extension classification', 'computations.md §11 explains the variance of the kernel and tangent sequence', 'A',
  'Grothendieck1966 p.102 discusses the de Rham extension and its group-theoretic interpretation; Coleman1998 §2, especially Theorem2.2, gives the universal vector-extension realization. The exact base hypotheses in Coleman’s paper are retained rather than expanded to an arbitrary nonreduced base.',
  'Grothendieck letter14October1963, added notesNovember1963, publication1966; Coleman1998',
  'Established for abelian varieties; this leaf concerns c54 external context. It is not the universal extension of the dual with the same kernel.', ['G66','Coleman1998'])
C(287,[265,266],54,[3,5], '2611–2652;2682–2694', 't_E=H¹(B); ... transposée de S_A',
  'The tangent sequence of E(A) identifies with the Hodge–de Rham extension for B; equivalently invariant one-forms of E(A) identify with H¹_dR(A). The canonical duality between H¹_dR(A) and H¹_dR(B) makes the two Hodge sequences transposes, exchanging H⁰Ω¹ with H¹O of the dual.',
  'stated as a claim to prove, initially qualified at least in characteristic0', 'computations.md §11 follows tangent/cotangent variance; no accidental interchange of A and B', 'B',
  'Coleman1998 Theorems1.1 and2.2, pp.1379–1384, compare the curvature, Chern-class and vectorial pairings and identify invariant forms with de Rham cohomology. His base assumption is reduced or flat over Z. Oda1969 Corollary5.11 handles the perfect-field characteristic-p realization.',
  'G66 firstcirculation1963/publication1966; Oda dissertation1967/publication1969; Coleman1998',
  'Established in these precise settings. H¹_dR(A) is naturally dual to Lie E(A), not identically its tangent space without switching to B.', ['G66','Coleman1998','Oda1969'])
C(288,[266],54,[5], '2685–2694', 'H^p(A,Ω_A^q) ... dual de H^q(B,Ω_A^p)',
  'The canonical degree-one dualities extend through exterior powers to a perfect pairing H^p(A,Ω_A^q)×H^q(B,Ω_B^p)→k. The displayed Ω_A on the B-cohomology factor is ill-typed. The page sketches the exterior-algebra reduction; it does not identify the whole de Rham filtration merely from a vector-space duality.',
  'proof sketched by exterior powers; one displayed base subscript is inconsistent', 'computations.md §11 derives the pairings as exterior tensor pairings', 'E',
  'Coleman1998 Theorem2.2(i) recalls all higher de Rham groups as exterior powers, and its duality comparison supplies the degree-one inputs. The bigraded coherent statement itself is the standard cohomology algebra of abelian varieties, not a new general Hodge theorem.',
  'Classical abelian-variety cohomology; exact opened exposition1998',
  'Established with Ω_B. The scan reading remains unaltered; this is a separately recorded mathematical alarm.', ['Coleman1998'])
C(289,[267],54,[5,7], '2696–2725', 'F^A et F^B ... orthogonaux',
  'Over a field of characteristic p, A[p] and B[p] are Cartier dual. The relative Frobenius kernels A[F] and B[F] have rank p^g and are orthogonal in the Weil pairing, giving0→A[F]→A[p]→D(B[F])→0. The proof uses F and V as dual maps, with the intervening Frobenius twists retained.',
  'proof sketched by the two transposed F–V sequences', 'computations.md §11 checks exactness, ranks and orthogonal complements from Cartier duality', 'A',
  'Grothendieck1966 p.102 writes the same characteristic-p exact sequence as motivation for the de Rham question. Oda1969 Corollary5.11 proves compatibility with the Hodge sequence under Dieudonné realization. The end A^(p) and B^(p) objects are not discarded by notation.',
  'G66 circulated1963, published1966; Oda1967 thesis/1969 publication',
  'Established over the stated characteristic-p field; exact isomorphism types still depend on Frobenius twists.', ['G66','Oda1969'])
C(290,[268],54,[7], '2727–2730', 'extension de p-algèbres de Lie ... puissance p-ième nulle',
  'The Hodge extension obtained from the Lie algebra of a universal vector extension is an extension of abelian restricted Lie algebras. Its vector subgroup has zero restricted p-operation. A finite-dimensional restricted Lie algebra in characteristic p determines a finite infinitesimal group scheme of height at most1; this alone does not recover the full Dieudonné module of A[p].',
  'asserted without proof as the bridge to the final question', 'computations.md §11 separates restricted Lie data from the two Dieudonné operators', 'E',
  'SGA3 VIIb Theorem4.4 and Corollary4.4.1, reedition pp.581–582 (original549–550), state the restricted-Lie/height-one equivalence. Oda1969 shows that the full A[p] realization has both F and V, a stronger object.',
  'Classical height-one theory before/withinSGA3; publication1970; Oda1969',
  'Established in characteristic p over a field, with finite-dimensionality for finite group schemes and zero p-operation only on the vector kernel.', ['SGA3VIIb','Oda1969'])
C(291,[269],54,[7], '2730', 'Cette extension peut-elle se déduire de ... p^A ??',
  'For an abelian variety A over a perfect field k of characteristic p, its finite group scheme A[p] canonically determines H¹_dR(A) as a Dieudonné module, with its Hodge exact sequence. In Oda’s convention M is contravariant from finite commutative p-primary group schemes to left Dieudonné modules.',
  'open on the pages as an explicit question', 'No numerical computation; the crucial check is variance and preservation of F,V and the Hodge subspace', 'B',
  'Oda1969 Corollary5.11, p.131, states exactly M(A[p])≅H¹_dR(A) and compatibility with the canonical exact sequence; introductory p.64 and Definition3.12 specify anti-equivalence. Corollary5.12 gives only an injection for general smooth proper X with a rational point, whose image is the infinitely closed forms; it is not the same full theorem.',
  'Harvard dissertationJune1967; journal1969; Oda creditsG66, circulated1963/published1966',
  'Settled affirmatively for abelian varieties over perfect fields. Do not generalize to all smooth proper varieties, arbitrary bases, or a category of crystalline motives.', ['Oda1969','G66'])
C(292,[270],54,[6], '2707–2710', 'Proposition A ... Y≠∅ ... fermé Z',
  'For f:X→Y of finite presentation with Y nonempty and F finitely presented on X, after replacing Y by a suitable nonempty open there is a finitely presented closed Z⊂Y such that every base change F_(Y′) is flat over Y′ exactly when Y′→Y factors through Z. After choosing Y affine and a finite affine cover U_i of X and shrinking further, the corresponding modules over every affine flattening base change are free. Y is not assumed integral or noetherian in the statement.',
  'proof sketched by noetherian approximation, generic freeness modulo nilpotents, and a referenced flattening-ideal lemma', 'computations.md §12 supplies the coefficient-ideal proof of the missing nilpotent lemma and the finite affine-cover sum; no pointwise replacement of the universal quantifier', 'A',
  'The mechanism is Grothendieck’s flattening theory. Stacks051R gives the precise generic-freeness input; Stacks0525 gives universal flattening over an Artinian base; Stacks05MZ Theorem38.24.1 gives a more general local flattening ideal with finite-generation conclusions. No exact published EGA proposition identical to this draft was identified; the full stated generic-open assertion is independently reconstructed from its proof.',
  'EGA-era flattening theory1960s; current exact component sources checked2026',
  'Established generic-open assertion with finite presentation. The earlier notes’ integral/noetherian restriction understates the leaf; those assumptions occur only after reduction in its proof.', ['Stacks051R','Stacks0525','Stacks05MZ'])
C(293,[270],54,[4], '2658–2677', 'M_p plat ... M_f libre; idéalement séparé',
  'The first cancelled corollary says that for SpecA irreducible, B finitely presented over A and M finitely presented over B, flatness of M at the unique minimal prime spreads after inverting f outside that prime to freeness over A_f. The subsequent cancelled flatness criterion changes a conclusion about M to conclusions about M/I^(n+1)M; its surviving variant requires nilpotence or noetherian ideal separation, and the undefined/uncertain antecedents prevent treating it as one unconditional flatness test.',
  'corrected and partly withdrawn in the same text; preserve the separate layers', 'computations.md §12 proves the first corollary from CL-SA07-292; the incompletely specified later criterion is not supplied with an invented proof', 'C',
  'Stacks051R and0525 plus the coefficient-ideal argument justify the first corollary. Stacks00MB/00MC cover precise completion exactness/faithfulness, but do not by themselves identify the draft’s ideally separated criterion. Searches for the exact phrase and flattening context did not recover the missing numbered4.1 statement.',
  'Classical generic-flatness development1960s; exact later criterion unlocated',
  'The generic-freeness corollary is settled; the later criterion needs its antecedent definitions and cancelled/live scope recovered. No blanket false verdict is inferred from cancellation.', ['Stacks051R','Stacks0525','Stacks00MB','Stacks00MC'], 'medium')
C(294,[279],54,[8], '2740–2750', 'C est l’intersection ... murs; corollaires2,3',
  'For a locally finite arrangement of affine real hyperplanes, a chamber is determined by the open half-spaces of its walls; every hyperplane contains a wall shared by two chambers; the relative interior of a wall is a chamber of the induced arrangement and is adjacent to exactly those two chambers. The final proof continues beyond the supplied leaf.',
  'proof sketched in an explicitly reused typescript; final continuation absent', 'computations.md §12 gives the finite-segment reduction and local transverse-neighbourhood proof', 'E',
  'Exact-phrase searches of the Bourbaki root-system/hyperplane-arrangement context did not securely identify the edition/page of this typescript. The geometric assertions are independently checked from local finiteness; SGA3XXI is only a root-system comparison and is not falsely cited as the source of these exact corollaries.',
  'Classical chamber geometry; exact reused edition unlocated',
  'Established elementary chamber facts. The supplied text proves only the visible part of its last corollary; authorship and original pagination remain external-context issues.', [], 'high')
C(295,[272],44,[2], '2787–2799', 'N(ξ) ... noyau ... équivalence d’Albanese',
  'The programme identifies a kernel N(ξ) inside Alb(S) for a family of algebraically trivial cycles and proposes that the induced quotient represents the relevant equivalence quotient. Since Albanese equivalence is not defined on these leaves, the strongest safe statement is a proposed universal regular quotient, not a proved equality with rational or algebraic equivalence.',
  'open on the pages, labelled Th.1 but called largely unproved in the letter', 'No computation can identify an omitted equivalence relation', 'C',
  'Kahn2021 Theorem1 and discussion of Murre’s criterion, pp.1–4 of the author version, show that algebraic representatives exist precisely when dimensions of surjective regular targets are bounded; he repairs the torsion-surjectivity gap in codimension2. ACMV2023 Theorem6.1 develops this functorially. These are exact modern comparisons, not an identification of the undefined manuscript relation.',
  'Murre1985; Kahn letter5December2018, publication2021; ACMVfirst2019/publication2023',
  'Partly realized by algebraic representatives. The manuscript’s N(ξ) and quotient must remain a reconstruction until its missing definitions are found.', ['Kahn2021','ACMV2023'], 'medium')
C(296,[273],44,[2,8], '2793;2974', 'dim P_ξ≤½b_(2i−1)',
  'The programme seeks a uniform bound dim P_ξ≤b_(2i−1)(X)/2 for all families of codimension-i cycles. Such a bound would force existence of the proposed universal regular quotient. It is verified in the modern theory for i=1,2,dimX via Tate-module/Bloch-map arguments, not by formal finite-dimensionality of every cycle group.',
  'open on the pages as Th.2 and reiterated conjecturally in the letter', 'computations.md §13: rank T_ℓA=2dimA converts the established injection into the dimension bound', 'C',
  'Kahn2021 pp.2–4 bounds codimension2 regular targets using Merkurjev–Suslin torsion control and fixes the surjectivity gap. ACMV2023 Proposition8.2, p.31, gives an injection after choosing a miniversal cycle and primes coprime to its multiplier for i=1,2,dimX. No general all-codimension bound is proved by those results.',
  'Murre1985; repaired2018/2021; functorial formulation2019/2023',
  'Established in the stated codimensions; the all-codimension assertion is not settled by these sources.', ['Kahn2021','ACMV2023'])
C(297,[275],44,[2,8], '2798–2803;2974', 'J^1=Pic^0; J^n=Alb^0; duales ...',
  'For smooth projective connected X over an algebraically closed field, the proposed J^i should be abelian varieties representing algebraically trivial cycles modulo the still-undefined Albanese equivalence, with endpoint identifications Pic⁰ and Alb and canonical complementary duality J^i↔J^(n+1−i). Modern universal regular representatives exist for i=1,2,n; this does not establish the claimed all-i canonical duality.',
  'open on the pages; letter explicitly says little has been proved', 'No computation replaces existence or a specified duality morphism', 'C',
  'ACMV2023 Theorems6.1,7.1,7.9 and9.5 give existence/endpoints and the characteristic-zero comparison with algebraic intermediate Jacobians in i=1,2,n. Ottem–Suzuki2020 Corollary4.2, p.9, shows that the usual Abel–Jacobi target need not itself be universal in codimension3 even when a representative exists. Kahn2021 Conjecture1 exhibits an explicit higher-codimension nonexistence expectation, not a proved counterexample.',
  'Picard/Albanese known before1964; Murre1985; Ottem–Suzuki first2019/published2020; ACMV2019/2023',
  'Partly developed; no all-codimension existence/duality theorem matching the full programme was found. The integral Abel–Jacobi identification has a known failure in higher codimension.', ['ACMV2023','Kahn2021','OttemSuzuki2020'])
C(298,[277],44,[2,8,9], '2803;2979–2995', 'A prouver ... positive ... polarisation',
  'The symmetric divisor correspondence obtained from intersections of middle-codimension families is hoped to descend to a positive polarization on the proposed abelian quotient. Symmetry and nondegeneracy only produce a symmetric isogeny to the dual; ampleness is an additional requirement. The page itself notes that primitive decomposition must be taken into account.',
  'open on the pages; sign and primitive qualifications are explicitly unsettled', 'computations.md §13 distinguishes symmetric isogeny from polarization and records the missing primitive/sign conventions', 'C',
  'Grothendieck1969 pp.194–197 and Kleiman1968 §§3.2–3.3 formulate the Hodge-type positivity conjecture with primitive signs. ACMV2025 establishes canonical autodualities for specified geometrically stably rational threefolds and studies their relation to polarizations; it is not an unconditional theorem for all i or all characteristics. The ordinary complex Hodge–Riemann theorem does not alone identify this undefined quotient.',
  'Classical Hodge positivity; Grothendieck1969; specified algebraic-representative advances2025',
  'Partly developed; general algebraic positivity in positive characteristic remains conjectural. No proof of ampleness is extracted from a symmetric correspondence alone.', ['Grothendieck1969','Kleiman1968','ACMV2025'])
C(299,[276],44,[4], '2850–2874', '〈ζ,ζ′〉=(−1)^m Z·Z′',
  'For a pencil of hyperplane sections with geometrically integral fibres and generic fibre Y, the programme compares the Néron–Tate pairing of restricted algebraically trivial cycles of complementary codimensions i+j=dimY+1 with an intersection on the total space. The literal formula still lacks a uniform meaning of m outside the middle case and a specification of vertical corrections/admissible lifts.',
  'open on the pages; a more general fibration statement and local variant are requested', 'computations.md §13 checks codimension: i+j=n=dimY+1; no unjustified numerical height calculation is claimed', 'C',
  'Kahn2024 Refined height pairing, Theorem4.14 in accepted version p.31, constructs a pairing on admissible subgroups over function fields, with coefficients inverting the exponential characteristic; Theorem5.6 includes algebraically trivial cycles and Theorem5.10 records an explicit negative-sign comparison for elliptic curves. Künnemann2001 Theorem1.6/Corollary1.7 constructs heights for all abelian varieties over number fields via lifts orthogonal to vertical cycles. Neither source validates the leaf’s unqualified sign/normalization.',
  'Künnemann2001; Kahn first1September2020, acceptedversionDecember2023, publication2024',
  'A substantial general height theory exists, but the precise pencil identity needs admissible lifts and fixed signs; it is not proved merely by the formal intersection number.', ['KahnHeight2024','Kunnemann2001'])
C(300,[275],44,[4], '2876–2884', 'Lefschetz ... isomorphisme; facteur fixe; surjectif',
  'For the proposed P^i, restriction to a generic hyperplane section Y of n-dimensional X is predicted to be an isomorphism if2i≤n−1, to identify the fixed part if2i=n, and to be surjective if2i≥n+1. The deleted finite-kernel assertion is not retained. These are assertions about the proposed cycle representatives, not just ordinary cohomology.',
  'open on the pages with a deleted stronger assertion', 'computations.md §13 compares the degree2i−1 thresholds with weak Lefschetz and records why the cycle quotient is extra data', 'C',
  'Katz–Messing1974 Corollary(Ogus), p.76, gives weak Lefschetz for their reasonable cohomologies; ACMV2023 Theorems7.1,7.9,9.5 identify actual representatives in endpoint/codimension2 cases. Their combination does not prove the all-i quotient statement. Searches of modern algebraic-representative literature did not locate the exact all-codimension formulation.',
  'Classical weak Lefschetz; Katz–Messing received8August1973/published1974; representative theory1985–2023',
  'Conditional programme beyond its known endpoint/specified representative cases. The fixed-part and surjectivity assertions require exact quotient and lattice conventions.', ['KatzMessing1974','ACMV2023'], 'medium')
C(301,[278],44,[6], '2942–2944', 'équivalence numérique=τ-équivalence',
  'If τ-equivalence means that a nonzero integer multiple is algebraically equivalent to zero, numerical equivalence equals τ-equivalence for divisors (Matsusaka). The same assertion for every algebraic cycle on a smooth projective variety is false: a very general complex quintic threefold has non-torsion homologically trivial codimension2 cycles modulo algebraic equivalence, hence numerically trivial cycles that are not τ-trivial.',
  'stated without proof as Th.7, with a Matsusaka marginal attribution', 'computations.md §13: homologically trivial implies numerically trivial, while nonzero rational Griffiths group rules out τ-triviality', 'C',
  'Clemens1983 Theorem0.2 and Corollary0.3, pp.19–20, prove an infinite-dimensional rational Abel–Jacobi image and non-finite generation modulo algebraic equivalence for very general quintic threefolds. Kahn2024 Theorem5.6 explicitly uses Matsusaka only for divisors. The definition of τ is reconstructed and must be checked elsewhere before attributing a literal counterexample to an undefined notation.',
  'Matsusaka divisor theorem before1964; Griffiths earlier counterexamples; strongerClemens1983',
  'False in all codimensions under the standard τ meaning; true divisor case retained. If τ meant another relation, the manuscript statement is not identifiable without its definition.', ['Clemens1983','KahnHeight2024'])
C(302,[274],44,[8], '2974', 'T_ℓ(J^i) ... quotient d’un sous-module ... même un sous-module',
  'The letter first predicts a subquotient realization of T_ℓJ^i in H^(2i−1)(X,Z_ℓ(i)), then hopes for an injection. Modern constructions give a rational realization and, for i=1,2,n, injections after controlling miniversal-cycle denominators; a canonical integral inclusion in codimension2 requires stronger torsion comparison. Arbitrary all-i integral embeddings are not supplied.',
  'open on the pages, distinguishing the weaker subquotient from the stronger embedding', 'computations.md §13 records the multiplier r and why ℓ∤r matters', 'C',
  'ACMV2023 Proposition8.2, p.31, proves the injection for i=1,2,n and ℓ different from the characteristic and coprime to the miniversal multiplier r, with torsion removed from the cohomology target. ACMV2025 Proposition10.3, pp.50–51, proves the canonical torsion/Tate comparison in characteristic0 or under a cohomological diagonal-decomposition hypothesis; Definition10.5, p.52, keeps the general positive-characteristic condition explicit.',
  'ACMVfirst2019/publication2023; monograph submitted2020, accepted2023,published2025',
  'Partly established with exact codimension, characteristic, torsion and denominator hypotheses. It is not an unconditional all-codimension canonical Z_ℓ embedding.', ['ACMV2023','ACMV2025'])
C(303,[274],44,[8], '2974–2976', 't_(J^i)⊂H^i(X,Ω_X^(i−1))',
  'In characteristic0 the letter predicts a tangent-space inclusion into the Hodge piece H^i(X,Ω_X^(i−1)). For the algebraic part of an intermediate Jacobian, its level-one Hodge substructure gives the corresponding tangent realization, with a choice of covariant/contravariant and duality conventions; it does not identify a tangent space for an as-yet-unconstructed all-i representative.',
  'open on the pages; conjectural margin', 'computations.md §13 checks Hodge bidegree(i−1,i) and distinguishes Lie space from its dual', 'C',
  'ACMV2023 §9 and Theorems9.1,9.3,9.5 construct/descent the algebraic intermediate Jacobian over subfields of C and compare it with representatives in i=1,2,n. These exact results support the level-one Hodge realization, not the asserted integral equality or a positive-characteristic Hodge analogue.',
  'Classical intermediate-Jacobian theory; precise descent/relative results2019–2023',
  'Established for the specified algebraic intermediate-Jacobian object after variance is fixed; the general manuscript quotient remains conjectural.', ['ACMV2023'], 'medium')
C(304,[271],44,[3], '2809–2840', 'En tous cas ... gradués ... TF ... exact et fidèle',
  'For a noetherian nonnegatively graded ring with an adic degree filtration, completion is flat and is exact on finite modules; for finitely generated graded modules it identifies with the product of homogeneous pieces. Faithfulness of completion on all modules needs an additional Jacobson-ideal condition; the margin instead restricts to graded modules satisfying the undefined condition TF.',
  'bulk argument cancelled; exactness/faithfulness retained only for the stated graded TF subcategory', 'computations.md §12 proves the finite graded product description and explains why A/(x−1) is not a counterexample inside a graded subcategory', 'E',
  'Stacks00MB Lemma10.97.2 proves noetherian completion flatness/exactness on finite modules; Stacks00MC Lemma10.97.3 gives faithful flatness for I⊂Jac(A). These precise results settle the ordinary completion inputs but do not define the manuscript TF condition.',
  'Classical completion theory before/aroundthe manuscript; exact current sources checked2026',
  'Established finite graded statement. The broader TF claim remains unidentifiable without its definition; cancellation does not authorize an unrestricted version.', ['Stacks00MB','Stacks00MC'], 'medium')
C(305,[],44,[5], '2893–2915', 'le s_a ... unique ... forme quad.',
  'For a finite spanning reduced root configuration in a rational vector space, a reflection preserving the roots and reversing a given root is orthogonal for the invariant positive form obtained by summing squared evaluations over the roots on the dual space. This determines the reflection uniquely. Integral coroot coefficients additionally use the root-lattice and crystallographic/primitive-root argument, not just positivity of a form.',
  'cancelled root-system draft with a proof sketch', 'computations.md §12 proves positivity, invariance and uniqueness; keeps the integral-lattice argument separate', 'E',
  'SGA3XXI Lemma1.2.1 and Corollaries1.2.3–1.2.8, reedition pp.65–66, give the canonical sum-of-squares form and finiteness in root-data language. The exact lattice claim is compared with Definition1.1.1; root-lattice assumptions on the leaf are narrower than an arbitrary reductive root datum.',
  'Classical root theory; SGA3publication1970, exact reedition opened2026',
  'Established finite root-system construction with its hypotheses; this is external root-data material, not a motivic positivity result.', ['SGA3XXI'])
C(306,[],44,[5], '2918–2935', 'P,P′ ... accouplés à Z ... 〈α′,α〉=2',
  'The draft packages roots and coroots in two integral lattices with rationally perfect pairing, paired roots having value2 and the two reflections preserving their respective root sets. The two lattices are generated by roots and coroots here; this is not automatically the same as a perfect integral character/cocharacter pairing for every reductive group.',
  'cancelled definition with the essential value2 added in the margin', 'computations.md §12 checks transpose reflections and distinguishes rational from integral perfectness', 'A',
  'SGA3XXI Definition1.1.1, Lemma1.1.4 and Corollary1.1.5, pp.63–64, state root data and their duality with explicit dual lattices. The published concept is a close developed counterpart; the broader integral lattice conventions are a genuine change of scope.',
  'SGA3notes1960s; publication1970',
  'Developed in the published root-datum theory, with the pairing distinction recorded rather than silently identifying the two definitions.', ['SGA3XXI'])
